menu "MagTag Timer"

    config MAGTAG_SYNC_LED_FEEDBACK
        bool "NeoPixel feedback during NTP sync"
        default y
        help
            Show blue on the NeoPixels while WiFi is up and SNTP sync is in
            progress, and blink red three times if the sync fails. Useful
            during bring-up/debugging; disable for production builds
            (CONFIG_MAGTAG_SYNC_LED_FEEDBACK=n in sdkconfig.defaults).

    config MAGTAG_MQTT_URI
        string "MQTT broker URI for Home Assistant (empty = disabled)"
        default ""
        help
            e.g. mqtt://homeassistant.local:1883 — the device publishes
            stats/discovery and reads retained config during its periodic
            network windows. Prefer setting NVS_DEFAULT_MQTT_URI in
            include/credentials.local.h (gitignored) so broker credentials
            never land in a committed sdkconfig.defaults. Empty disables
            the MQTT session entirely.

    config MAGTAG_MQTT_USER
        string "MQTT username"
        default ""
        depends on MAGTAG_MQTT_URI != ""

    config MAGTAG_MQTT_PASS
        string "MQTT password"
        default ""
        depends on MAGTAG_MQTT_URI != ""

    config MAGTAG_WEEKDAY_MIN
        int "Weekday screen-time allocation (minutes)"
        default 60
        range 1 1440
        help
            Daily allocation on weekdays. Seeds NVS; changing this (or any
            allocation) reseeds NVS defaults on the next boot automatically
            (the stored stamp is a fingerprint of these values).

    config MAGTAG_WEEKEND_MIN
        int "Weekend screen-time allocation (minutes)"
        default 120
        range 1 1440
        help
            Daily allocation on Saturdays and Sundays.

    config MAGTAG_HOLIDAY_MIN
        int "Holiday screen-time allocation (minutes)"
        default 120
        range 1 1440
        help
            Daily allocation on dates in the NVS holiday list.

    config MAGTAG_SUMMER_MIN
        int "Summer-break screen-time allocation (minutes)"
        default 120
        range 1 1440
        help
            Weekday allocation during school summer break (outside the
            school year defined in nvs_defaults.h). Weekends and listed
            holidays keep their own allocations.

    config MAGTAG_BREAK_INTERVAL_MIN
        int "Eye-rest break interval (minutes of running time)"
        default 30
        range 0 480
        help
            After this many minutes of accumulated RUNNING time, the timer
            auto-pauses into an enforced Screen Break. 0 disables eye-rest
            breaks entirely.

    config MAGTAG_BREAK_DURATION_MIN
        int "Eye-rest break duration (minutes)"
        default 15
        range 1 120
        help
            Length of the enforced Screen Break. When it ends the device
            chimes and waits in PAUSED for a manual resume (Button B).

    comment "Extra timers (Button C swaps; empty name disables a slot)"

    config MAGTAG_TIMER1_NAME
        string "Extra timer 1 name"
        default ""
        help
            Display name of extra countdown timer 1 (e.g. "Piano"). Keep it
            short (~12 chars) — it renders at 12 pt next to the completion
            counter and duration. Empty disables the slot. Extra timers are
            plain countdowns: no eye-rest breaks of their own, same alerts
            and NTP behaviour as the Screen timer, and usable during a
            Screen Break. Button C cycles between enabled timers (blocked
            only while one is RUNNING).

    config MAGTAG_TIMER1_MIN
        int "Extra timer 1 duration (minutes)"
        default 15
        range 1 1440
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER1_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER1_RELOADABLE
        bool "Extra timer 1 reloadable"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER1_NAME != ""
        help
            Button B resets this timer to full (same day) whenever it is not
            RUNNING. The mode line then shows how often it ran to completion
            today, e.g. "Piano (x2) - 15 min".

    config MAGTAG_TIMER1_BREAK_ELIGIBLE
        bool "Extra timer 1 is break-eligible"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER1_NAME != ""
        help
            This timer is a genuine break from a screen (music practice,
            reading), so it may be STARTED during a Screen Break and its
            running time DRAINS the screen-exposure balance 1:1 instead of
            feeding it. Leave it off for chores done with the TV on
            (laundry folding): those add exposure and cannot be started
            during a break. Non-eligible timers should also be
            RELOADABLE=n, or the chore can be re-earned without doing it.

    config MAGTAG_TIMER2_NAME
        string "Extra timer 2 name"
        default ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER2_MIN
        int "Extra timer 2 duration (minutes)"
        default 15
        range 1 1440
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER2_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER2_RELOADABLE
        bool "Extra timer 2 reloadable"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER2_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER2_BREAK_ELIGIBLE
        bool "Extra timer 2 is break-eligible"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER2_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER3_NAME
        string "Extra timer 3 name"
        default ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER3_MIN
        int "Extra timer 3 duration (minutes)"
        default 15
        range 1 1440
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER3_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER3_RELOADABLE
        bool "Extra timer 3 reloadable"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER3_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER3_BREAK_ELIGIBLE
        bool "Extra timer 3 is break-eligible"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER3_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER4_NAME
        string "Extra timer 4 name"
        default ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER4_MIN
        int "Extra timer 4 duration (minutes)"
        default 15
        range 1 1440
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER4_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER4_RELOADABLE
        bool "Extra timer 4 reloadable"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER4_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_TIMER4_BREAK_ELIGIBLE
        bool "Extra timer 4 is break-eligible"
        default n
        depends on MAGTAG_TIMER4_NAME != ""

    config MAGTAG_MAX_AWAKE_SEC
        int "Awake failsafe: force deep sleep after (seconds)"
        default 180
        range 30 600
        help
            Last-resort battery protection: if a wake is still running
            after this many seconds (WiFi driver hang, stuck BUSY line,
            firmware bug), the device saves the timer snapshot and forces
            deep sleep. The longest legitimate wake (final-minute watch +
            expiry alarm + refreshes) is ~2 minutes; keep a margin above
            that.

    config MAGTAG_IDLE_SYNC_INTERVAL_MIN
        int "NTP sync interval while IDLE (minutes)"
        default 60
        range 5 1440
        help
            How often the clock re-syncs while the timer is IDLE. The
            ESP32-S2 has no crystal-backed RTC — deep-sleep timekeeping
            runs on an RC oscillator that can drift minutes per day, so
            long intervals make the displayed clock visibly wrong.
            RUNNING keeps its own fixed 10-minute cadence.

    config MAGTAG_RUNNING_SYNC_INTERVAL_MIN
        int "NTP sync interval while RUNNING (minutes)"
        default 10
        range 1 120
        help
            Clock-recheck cadence while the countdown runs. The S2's
            RC-oscillator sleep drift makes this the accuracy bound of the
            displayed countdown and the expiry moment.

    config MAGTAG_EXPIRY_ALARM_CYCLES
        int "Expiry alarm length (beep cycles, ~3 s each)"
        default 5
        range 1 20
        help
            3-beep cycles the TIME'S UP alarm plays unless dismissed.

    config MAGTAG_BREAK_ALARM_CYCLES
        int "Break alarm length (beep cycles, ~2.2 s each)"
        default 3
        range 1 20
        help
            2-beep cycles the Screen Break alarm plays unless silenced.

    config MAGTAG_ALERT_VOLUME_PCT
        int "Alert volume (percent; >100 adds clipping gain)"
        default 200
        range 0 200
        help
            Volume for all alert audio (expiry/break/bed tones and the
            custom WAV). 100 = the clean reference amplitude; values
            above 100 apply digital gain that clips at the DAC rails —
            louder and harsher through the small speaker, approaching
            the old square-wave loudness at 200. 0 mutes. Also editable
            from Home Assistant (number entity), which overrides this
            default.

    config MAGTAG_STATUS_LED_BRIGHTNESS
        int "Status NeoPixel brightness (percent)"
        default 100
        range 5 100
        help
            Percent scale applied to status pixels (state colour, WiFi sync
            feedback, final-countdown binary display). Alert pulses (expiry
            red, break cyan) are NOT scaled — they accompany alarms.

    config MAGTAG_STATUS_LED_ACK_HOLD_MS
        int "NeoPixel ack hold (ms)"
        default 400
        range 0 3000
        help
            The PRE-PRESS HOLD: how long the old colour stays up before
            the pixel that acknowledges a press changes. The
            acknowledgement is the transition, so there has to be a frame
            of the old state for the new one to be a change from. Shared
            by Button B's start/resume and the chore ack, which are the
            same gesture read by the same person. Wants to be SHORT: it is
            dead time in front of the first ack.

            THIS USED TO BE ONE FIGURE WITH TWO JOBS, and M2-T15 split it.
            It was also the chore-ack coalescing window — how long the
            panel waits for the next press before it paints — and the two
            pull in opposite directions: the hold wants to be short, the
            window long. Tied together, a natural pause between two
            presses outlasted the window, the panel started painting
            mid-gesture, and the press that landed during that paint cost
            a second refresh. The window is now
            MAGTAG_CHORE_PAINT_QUIET_MS, below.

            400 IS NOT A SWEPT VALUE — NOBODY HAS SWEPT THIS YET. What a
            board established on 2026-09-22 is a LOWER BOUND for the
            WINDOW, not for the hold: at the 250 this shipped with, a
            person could not get the second chore pressed before the
            refresh began. 400 was adopted then because design §2.5 had
            already estimated ~400 in prose. Now that the window has its
            own knob, that finding no longer says anything about this
            figure in either direction; it stays at 400 because nothing
            has been measured that would move it. See design §8 Q-C.

    config MAGTAG_CHORE_PAINT_QUIET_MS
        int "Chore-ack paint quiet window (ms)"
        default 1200
        range 100 10000
        help
            How long the panel waits, after the last chore ack that
            applied, before it paints. SLIDING: every ack that APPLIES
            grants another full window (a press the ack refuses, Button D
            on a two-row list, does not), so a gesture of several presses
            paints once, this long after its last press — as long as no
            gap between presses outlasts this window and the burst budget
            below has not run out; a gesture that spends the whole budget
            is painted as the budget allows, not once.

            THE NEOPIXELS ARE THE FAST CHANNEL AND THE PANEL IS THE SLOW
            ONE, which is why this can be long. Every press already gets
            an instant pixel flip, so nobody waits on the panel to learn
            their press registered; the panel does not need to be quick,
            it needs to be settled. The cost is honest and is paid on
            every checklist wake: a lone press reaches the glass this much
            later, and the device stays awake this much longer before it
            sleeps.

            1200 IS NOT A SWEPT VALUE. It is chosen to sit above a natural
            press-pause-press rhythm (the 400 this replaced was too short
            for one on the board, 2026-09-23) while keeping a lone press's
            paint about 2.4 s after the press at the defaults (400 ms
            hold + 1200 ms window + a ghost-cleaned partial ESTIMATED at
            ~0.8 s, not measured), plus the boot. Raise it if a board still
            sees a paint land mid-gesture; lower it if the lone-press
            latency reads as sluggish.

            THE MINIMUM IS NOT ZERO, on purpose: the coalescer's tail loop
            (wake_flow.c) repaints after a press made during a paint only
            once this window has closed, and each such window is what
            spends the burst budget below. A window of zero would let a pad
            that fires during every paint repaint without spending any.

            NOT EVERY COMBINATION WITH THE BUDGET BUILDS, and that is
            deliberate: the number of repaints grows as budget / window,
            which no range can express. wake_flow.c _Static_asserts that
            the worst case the two allow (hold + budget + every repaint
            they permit, each charged as a full refresh plus the driver's
            1 s minimum interval) stays under half of
            MAGTAG_MAX_AWAKE_SEC, so a short window with a long budget is
            refused at build time rather than forcing a failsafe sleep in
            the middle of a refresh.

    config MAGTAG_CHORE_ACK_BURST_MS
        int "Chore-ack burst budget (ms)"
        default 8000
        range 0 30000
        help
            The HARD BOUND on one gesture, in milliseconds the coalescer
            spends WAITING for presses after the first chore ack, summed
            over every quiet window of the wake. The window above is
            sliding, so something has to stop a pad that keeps producing
            edges, or a child leaning on a button, from holding a battery
            device awake indefinitely.

            PANEL TIME IS NOT COUNTED. The firmware has no millisecond
            clock to measure a refresh with, and does not need one: a
            repaint after a press made during a paint happens only after a
            full quiet window closes, and that window is spent from this
            budget, so repaints are bounded by the budget too. A pad that
            fires exactly once per paint and never otherwise — the worst
            case — gets max(2, ceil(budget / quiet window)) paints, and a
            Button A press that ends the gesture can add one more, once.
            At the defaults that is 8 s of waiting and at most 7 paints
            (8 with A). Each is an estimated ~0.8 s ghost-cleaned partial
            unless something makes it full (~3 s): the every-5th cadence
            WILL land on at least one of 7, and a break end or a mode
            toggle makes its paint full too. The build refuses any budget
            and window whose worst case would crowd
            CONFIG_MAGTAG_MAX_AWAKE_SEC (see the window's help above).

            IT REPLACED A COUNT OF WINDOWS, which was CHORE_MAX (3). The
            count looked like generosity — one window per row there is to
            tick — and was the opposite: a person ticking all three rows
            had spent the whole budget, so CORRECTING one of those presses
            was impossible and read in the field as "toggling a checkmark
            multiple times doesn't work". A budget does not shrink as the
            list gets longer, and it bounds the thing that actually costs
            power (awake milliseconds) instead of a proxy for it.

            8000, RAISED FROM 4000 WITH THE QUIET WINDOW, and it is sized
            against that window rather than against a guess at pace. A
            gesture paints once only if no gap between its presses
            outlasts the quiet window (the first gap may also run the
            pre-press hold, which the budget does not count), so the
            longest single-paint gesture of N presses spends at most N
            windows: one after each press. That is 4.8 s for three ticks
            and a correction at 1200, and 7.2 s for three ticks and three
            corrections. 4000 would cut the first of those off and paint
            mid-gesture — the very defect the window was raised to cure —
            and 8000 is the smallest round figure that holds the second.
            At a steady one press a second the four-press session spends
            3.8 s. It is NOT A SWEPT VALUE
            and must stay finite whatever a board says about comfort: its
            job is the flaky-pad bound.

            0 disables coalescing outright: the first ack still flips and
            still paints, and a press made during that paint still gets
            the one repaint the tail owes it — nothing after that.

    config MAGTAG_QUIET_START_HHMM
        int "NeoPixel quiet hours start (HHMM, 24h)"
        default 2230
        range 0 2359
        help
            Status NeoPixels (state pixel, WiFi sync feedback) stay dark
            from this local time until the quiet-hours end. Encoded as
            HHMM: 2230 = 10:30 PM. Set start == end to disable quiet
            hours. Alert pulses (expiry) are NOT suppressed — they
            accompany an audible alarm and are dismissable.

    config MAGTAG_QUIET_END_HHMM
        int "NeoPixel quiet hours end (HHMM, 24h)"
        default 800
        range 0 2359
        help
            Local time the status NeoPixels come back. 800 = 8:00 AM. A
            window with start > end wraps midnight.

    config MAGTAG_BEDTIME_HHMM
        int "Bed time (HHMM, 24h; 0 disables)"
        default 2200
        range 0 2359
        help
            From this local evening time until day rollover (midnight)
            the device shows a non-dismissable Bed Time screen, disables
            button wakes, and sleeps in ~2 h chunks waking only for NTP
            sync and the rollover check. 0 disables the feature. Values
            other than 0 must be 1800-2359 — anything else is treated as
            the 2200 default at load (a daytime bedtime would lock the
            device out for the whole day). Also editable from Home
            Assistant, which is the only remote fix path while locked.

    comment "Over-the-air firmware updates"

    config MAGTAG_OTA_URL
        string "OTA manifest URL (empty = OTA disabled)"
        default ""
        help
            HTTPS endpoint serving the update manifest (the JSON listing
            the available firmware version and image URL). Plain http is
            rejected at every entry point — an unauthenticated firmware
            endpoint is an arbitrary-code-execution channel, and TLS is
            what makes the manifest trustworthy.

            Prefer setting NVS_DEFAULT_OTA_URL in
            include/credentials.local.h (gitignored) so a hosting
            endpoint never lands in a committed sdkconfig.defaults —
            the same treatment MAGTAG_MQTT_URI gets.

            This is only the fallback the device uses while NVS holds no
            endpoint of its own. It is deliberately NOT part of the
            defaults fingerprint, so once Home Assistant has set an
            endpoint (the "OTA manifest URL" text entity, or the bulk
            config document's ota_url), changing this value no longer
            affects that device. Empty disables update checks entirely.

    config MAGTAG_OTA_CHECK_ON_SYNC
        bool "Also check for updates on a Button D full sync"
        default n
        help
            By default an update check runs only at the day rollover,
            when the device is already awake and nobody is waiting on it.
            Enable this to also check during the manual full sync Button D
            forces — useful while testing an update, at the cost of a
            slower Button D and the extra radio time on every press.

            Like the URL, this only seeds the runtime value: the "OTA
            check on sync" switch in Home Assistant overrides it, and a
            menuconfig change here will not revert that.

    config MAGTAG_OTA_MIN_BATT_PCT
        int "Battery floor for an update check (percent)"
        default 30
        range 0 100
        help
            Skip the update check when the battery is below this. A
            download is the longest radio session the device ever runs,
            and a brownout partway through a flash write is the one
            failure this design cannot recover from cheaply — so the
            check does not even start on a low battery. It simply runs at
            the next rollover after a charge. 0 disables the check
            (not recommended on battery power).

    config MAGTAG_OTA_MAX_SEC
        int "Download window budget (seconds)"
        default 300
        range 60 600
        help
            Awake-failsafe extension applied before the download window
            opens, and the deadline the download loop checks itself
            against. Exceeding it aborts cleanly — the partial image is
            discarded and the boot partition is never switched — instead
            of the awake failsafe killing the write mid-flight.

            Kept at or below 600 to stay consistent with
            MAGTAG_MAX_AWAKE_SEC's own range: this value temporarily
            replaces that failsafe, so a budget beyond its ceiling would
            mean OTA quietly runs with weaker battery protection than the
            rest of the firmware is allowed to.

    config MAGTAG_OTA_MAX_FAILS
        int "Consecutive failures before giving up on a version"
        default 3
        range 1 10
        help
            Retry budget, counted per target version. An image that
            reliably fails to download (too slow a link, a bad host)
            would otherwise be re-attempted at every rollover forever —
            a daily battery cost that produces nothing and looks, from
            the outside, like nothing is happening. After this many
            consecutive failures against the SAME version the device
            stops trying and reports it to Home Assistant. Publishing a
            different version string resets the counter and re-arms it.

            The range floor of 1 is deliberate: it makes 0 unreachable
            from configuration, which is what lets ota_policy treat a
            zero budget as "the caller forgot to set the field" and fall
            back to OTA_MAX_FAILS_DEFAULT rather than as "no retries" or
            "unlimited retries". Both of those readings are defensible in
            the abstract, which is exactly why the value must never
            arrive ambiguous. Widening this to 0 would silently change
            the meaning of a config nobody edited.

    config MAGTAG_OTA_MIN_FREE_HEAP
        int "Free-heap floor for an update check or download (bytes)"
        default 40960
        range 32768 131072
        help
            Both OTA gates refuse below this. The download needs roughly
            40 KB free during the TLS handshake and transfer (mbedTLS
            record buffers plus the http client), and a handshake that
            runs out of heap fails in a way that reads like a
            certificate problem — so the gate turns it into a logged
            low_heap instead.

            The floor of 32 KB is structural, not taste. With
            CONFIG_MBEDTLS_DYNAMIC_BUFFER unset, the record buffers alone
            are CONFIG_MBEDTLS_SSL_IN_CONTENT_LEN (16384) plus
            CONFIG_MBEDTLS_SSL_OUT_CONTENT_LEN (4096) = 20 KB, before the
            SSL context, the pinned CA, the peer chain and the http
            client's 1 KB rx buffer. A floor below that would configure a
            gate that PASSES at a heap level where the handshake is
            guaranteed to fail — the exact outcome this symbol exists to
            prevent — so the range stops short of it rather than trusting
            the operator not to go there. Widening it again means turning
            the dynamic-buffer option on first.

            In bytes rather than KB so app_main can pass the symbol
            straight into ota_flow_cfg_t without arithmetic: main.c is
            the composition root and a unit conversion there is a policy
            number wearing a cast.

            Board-tunable on purpose. The 2025 MagTag has 2 MB PSRAM,
            but CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND=y means a PSRAM-less board
            must still boot, and that board has the tighter headroom.
            Raise this if a download reports low_heap on a board that
            has the memory; lower it only with a measurement of
            esp_get_minimum_free_heap_size() across a real download.

endmenu
